```kotlin
sealed interface ProfileUiState {
    data object Loading : ProfileUiState
    data class Success(val user: User) : ProfileUiState
    data class Error(val message: String) : ProfileUiState
}

@Composable
fun ProfileScreen(state: ProfileUiState) {
    when (state) {
        is ProfileUiState.Loading -> LoadingSpinner()
        is ProfileUiState.Success -> ProfileContent(state.user)   // state.user is smart-cast, no cast needed
        is ProfileUiState.Error -> ErrorMessage(state.message)
        // no else needed — the compiler knows these three cases are exhaustive
    }
}
```

Notice `state.user` is accessible with no explicit cast inside the `Success` branch — Kotlin's smart-casting narrows `state`'s type to `ProfileUiState.Success` within that branch automatically, since the `when` already proved which subtype it is. `data object` (rather than a plain `object`) is used for `Loading` specifically to get a sensible `toString()`/`equals()` for a case with no data — a small but real ergonomic detail that avoids `object`'s default identity-based equality behaving surprisingly in tests or logging.
